---
components: ["FunnelCompare"]
description: Funnel compare sets two or more funnels side by side, stage by stage, so a channel, segment, or test arm can be read against another.
---

<script>
  import { FunnelCompare } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const organic = [
    { id: "visit", label: "Visitors", value: 10000 },
    { id: "signup", label: "Signup", value: 6000 },
    { id: "activate", label: "Activated", value: 3000 },
    { id: "paid", label: "Paid", value: 1200 },
  ];
  const paid = [
    { id: "visit", label: "Visitors", value: 8000 },
    { id: "signup", label: "Signup", value: 3000 },
    { id: "activate", label: "Activated", value: 1500 },
    { id: "paid", label: "Paid", value: 640 },
  ];
  const referral = [
    { id: "visit", label: "Visitors", value: 2400 },
    { id: "signup", label: "Signup", value: 1900 },
    { id: "activate", label: "Activated", value: 1400 },
    { id: "paid", label: "Paid", value: 700 },
  ];
  const channels = [
    { id: "organic", label: "Organic", stages: organic },
    { id: "paid", label: "Paid", stages: paid },
    { id: "referral", label: "Referral", stages: referral },
  ];
  const arms = [
    { id: "a", label: "Control", stages: organic, color: "neutral" },
    { id: "b", label: "New onboarding", stages: organic.map((stage, i) => ({ ...stage, value: Math.round(stage.value * [1, 1.02, 1.3, 1.4][i]) })) },
  ];

  let selected = null;
</script>

`FunnelCompare` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

It is one table: stages down the side, a column per funnel, and each funnel's overall conversion underneath. Stages line up by `id`, so a funnel that lacks a stage shows a dash there. For a single funnel, use [FunnelBars](/components/FunnelBars).

## Basic

Pass `funnels`, each with an `id`, a `label`, and its `stages`, and a `label` for the table. Bars are scaled against the largest stage of any funnel, so a bar's length means the same thing in every column.

<FunnelCompare funnels={channels} label="Signup funnel by channel" />

## Scale

Set `scale` to `"independent"` to scale each funnel against its own largest stage. The sizes no longer compare, but the shapes do, which is what an A/B test wants.

<FunnelCompare funnels={arms} scale="independent" label="Onboarding test, by arm" />

## Rate and format

Set `rate` to `"none"` to drop the overall conversion row. `format` takes `Intl.NumberFormat` options or a function.

<FunnelCompare funnels={channels} rate="none" format={{ notation: "standard" }} label="Signup funnel by channel, counts" />

## Color and size

Set `color` for every bar, or give a funnel its own `color`. `size` is `"sm"`, `"md"`, or `"lg"`.

<FunnelCompare funnels={arms} size="sm" label="Onboarding test, by arm" />

## Selectable

Set `selectable` to make each bar a toggle button. Bind `selected`, a funnel and stage id, or listen for `select`, whose detail carries the funnel and the stage with its statistics.

<FunnelCompare funnels={channels} selectable bind:selected label="Pick a stage" />

## Accessibility

The table is captioned with the `label`; each funnel is a column header and each stage a row header, so a cell reads as "Paid, Signup, 3K". The overall conversion row is a footer. Without a `label` the table is decorative. When `selectable`, the bars are toggle buttons named with their funnel, stage, and value, with arrow key navigation in reading order and a single tab stop.
